  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2024 by Peter Lang Verlag, <em>Digital Visual Art Education</em> addresses a gap in scholarly literature by proposing a unified framework for studying digital media in visual arts practice and pedagogy. The work aligns with the United Nations Sustainable Development Goal for Quality Education and sits within the established Visual Communication series, which has published interdisciplinary work on visual culture for over a decade. As a recent publication, citation-based impact signals are still emerging.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Robert C. H. Sweeny's monograph brings together theories of temporality and multilinearity to establish digital visual art as a legitimate field of scholarly inquiry. The book is positioned at the intersection of Media Studies, Studio Art, and Art and Design Education\u2014fields that have increasingly recognized the need for critical frameworks specific to how contemporary artists work with computational and digital tools. By anchoring digital visual art within established communication and media studies traditions, the work offers both practitioners and educators a theoretical vocabulary for analyzing how visual expression operates in the digital domain.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book appears in Volume 10 of Peter Lang's Visual Communication series, a sustained publishing program that has maintained focus on visual culture and communication for more than a decade. This series placement signals editorial alignment with an established scholarly community and suggests the work engages established conversations within visual communication scholarship rather than standing in isolation. The series has consistently published work spanning topics from visual rhetoric to digital culture to environmental communication, indicating a venue with sustained readership in media and communication disciplines.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work carries relevance to educational frameworks and policy, particularly given its emphasis on art and design education pedagogy. This alignment with Quality Education objectives reflects growing institutional interest in how digital literacy and creative practice intersect in formal learning environments. The book's focus on multidisciplinary approaches suggests it may serve as a reference text for educators designing curricula around digital media and visual culture.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">As a 2024 publication, this work is in its earliest phase of scholarly circulation. Traditional citation metrics and broader adoption patterns typically require 18\u201336 months to become visible in aggregated databases. The presence of the work in library cataloging systems and its publication by an established academic publisher provide baseline indicators of formal scholarly dissemination, though downstream evidence of teaching adoption, citation influence, and community engagement remains to be documented as the work reaches its audience.</p>",
